# Neon Pairs implementation

User requests the full spec 2.2 game, board codes, snapshots, replay CLI and offline `dist/index.html`, arranged as Architecture requires. The user explicitly requires planning first and a separate subagent for each task; the parent reviews and runs commands without editing code.

Task order: (1) toolchain and CLI wiring, (2) pure game core and unit tests, (3) browser session/timing/storage, (4) board/artwork/controls/React UI, (5) acceptance verification, fixes and final distribution. One subagent slot runs serially alongside the parent.

Toolchain task created exact T02 pins, compiler projects, build/test scripts and `scripts/replay.ts`. Pure game imports must use explicit `.ts` extensions because the replay command uses Node's native TypeScript stripping. Expected APIs: `src/game/replay.ts` exports `replayGame(text): GameState | null`; `src/game/snapshot.ts` exports `encodeSnapshot(state): string`. Those files are intentionally left to the core task. `src/main.tsx` is a temporary title-only mount for the later UI task.

There are separate app, Node and test compiler projects; the test project includes DOM/JSX types and `.test.ts`/`.test.tsx` files plus application e2e tests. Initial dependency installation finished; `package-lock.json` and `node_modules` exist. Parent confirmed every direct T02 pin and installed Chromium `156.0.8078.4`. A fresh `npm ci` and final acceptance checks remain to task 5.

Task 2 completed the pure core, with explicit `.ts` imports throughout. `src/game/state.ts` exports `createGame(code): GameState | null`, `activateCard(state,index)`, `advanceGame(state,milliseconds)`, `isWon`, `pairCount`, `isRevealed` and `formatTime`. `GameState` has immutable `board: {code,size,seed,deck}`, `matched`, `selection`, `moves`, `elapsedMs` and `started`. Selection kinds are `closed`, `one` (index), and `mismatch` (first,second,remainingMs). `src/game/boardCode.ts` exports `GRID_SIZES`, `GridSize`, `isGridSize`, `MAX_SEED`, `parseBoardCode`. `snapshot.ts` exports `encodeSnapshot` and `decodeSnapshot`; `replay.ts` exports `replayGame`.

Logical time accepts nonnegative finite fractional deltas and retains fractions. The adapter should synchronize monotonic active time on activation, export and visibility changes. Snapshot export floors elapsed and pending remaining ms, with remaining at least 1. The elapsed cap does not stop mismatch countdown. Pure decode validates formats but doesn't implement imported pause: task 3 must pause unfinished imports until Resume and prevent completed imports from best-score/celebration effects. Invalid core parsing returns null without browser mutation.

Task 2 verification: `npm test` passed 85 tests in four colocated files, covering literal independent generation vectors, rejection sampling, every size, transitions, caps, strict snapshot/replay consistency, UTF-8/size/action limits, equal-time replay boundary and large safe timeline values. `npm run typecheck` passed all projects. CLI smoke checks passed for valid JSON-only stdout/exit 0 and invalid empty stdout/stderr/nonzero. Largest core TypeScript file is 86 lines. Next is browser session under `src/game/session`; UI remains a temporary title-only mount until task 4.

This record is intentionally in the existing working-memory folder. Its existing gitignore pattern keeps the file outside Open Forge's admitted inventory; `index` reports no changes, and explicit indexing reports an unadmitted source. The boundary was left unchanged; read this known path directly during the active task.

Final acceptance task must replace README's pending validation paragraph with measured versions/results, record accessibility-tree evidence and say no screen reader was used unless one actually was. Keep all authored TypeScript files at or below 200 lines. No product specification edits are authorized or needed.

